  "response_complete": true,
  "response_text": "1. **Assess Your Needs**: Understand the nature and extent of your disability. This includes identifying any accommodations you may need to perform your job effectively.\n\n2. **Consult HR**: Reach out to your HR department to discuss your situation. They can provide guidance and help you understand your rights and responsibilities.\n\n3. **Prepare a Letter**: Write a clear and concise letter to your employer. Include details about your disability, the accommodations you need, and how it affects your ability to perform your job.\n\n4. **Discuss with Your Manager**: Have an open and honest conversation with your manager about your disability and the accommodations you need. Be prepared to discuss any concerns they may have and how you plan to address them.\n\n5. **Follow Up**: After discussing with your manager, follow up with them to ensure that you have the necessary accommodations and that your needs are being met.\n\n6. **Document Everything**: Keep a record of all communications with HR and your manager. This can be helpful if you need to escalate the issue or if you need to provide documentation for any legal or HR-related issues.\n\n7. **Stay Informed**: Stay informed about any changes in your disability status or any new accommodations that may be required. This can help you stay compliant with your employer's policies and ensure that you are receiving the support you need.",
